{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 28, :comments ["<b>My master Moses, restrain them</b> – The reason for this is that Moses spoke to the people the words of HaShem, that He would apportion from the spirit that was upon him and give it upon those standing with him at the Tent of Meeting. Now these who did not go out to the Tent and make themselves part of the assembly of those apportioned are like rebels against his words, and therefore he said to him <b>restrain them</b>, for perhaps a false spirit is in their mouths or an evil spirit terrifies them, and so they need to be placed in prison like a madman prophesying. And Moses in his humility replied: \"Would that all the people of HaShem were prophets, that HaShem would give His spirit upon them,\" for HaShem had given His spirit upon them without the apportioning of the spirit that was upon him, and would that this were so among all the people.\n\nBut from the words of our Sages it appears that the practice in Israel was that no man would prophesy of future things in the place of a prophet greater than himself, but they would follow after him as his disciples, and they are the sons of the prophets. Thus they said in Sanhedrin (17a): Granted, according to the one who says \"Moses died\" — that is why Joshua said \"My master Moses, restrain them.\" But according to the one who says it was concerning the affair of the quail, why \"restrain them\"? For it should have been like one ruling a law before his master. And likewise according to the one who says they prophesied concerning Gog and Magog. And so they further mentioned in connection with the matter of Deborah in tractate Megillah (14a). And Moses said that he is the master and forgave his honor, and desired and rejoiced in this.\n\nAnd in the Jerusalem Targum I saw that he rendered \"restrain them\" as \"withhold the holy spirit from them.\" He derived it from the language of \"Behold, my lips I will not restrain\" (Psalms 40:10), \"Do not restrain Your mercies from me\" (ibid. verse 12). And the reason of the matter is that Joshua thought, since they are among the written ones [i.e., the listed elders], they are prophesying because the spirit of Moses was apportioned upon all the written ones, all of whom he intended. And therefore he said to him: it is not fitting that they should prophesy in the camp, for they did not fulfill the word of HaShem that He commanded, \"And you shall take them with you to the Tent of Meeting, and they shall stand there with you\" (Numbers 11:16). And now it will appear that the prophecy of all of them is not from the apportioning of the spirit that is upon you, and it is fitting for my master to direct the spirit of Elohim to return it to you, so that it not be apportioned from you except to those who stand before you in the word of HaShem — for since you initially intended upon all the written ones, some of your splendor rested upon them, and if you now direct to withdraw it from them, it will rest upon those who stand before you alone, according to the word of HaShem. And Moses said that he would not be jealous, for he desires that they prophesy in his presence or outside his presence, since HaShem has given His spirit upon them through apportioning from Moses or without apportioning from him."], :book :numbers, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :notes {0 "The phrase כל הכתובים (\"all the written ones\") refers to the seventy elders listed by name; the sense is preserved literally. The citation of Numbers 11:16 within the comment is actually a composite/paraphrase of verses 16 and 24, as Ramban conflates the divine command with its execution."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:04:41.641972874Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}